\lispPackageChapter{candlesticks/data-source-kinds}

A data source kind is a classification of \emph{how Candlesticks talks to
a source}: api, website, feed, file, or manual.  A source row is ``the
CoinGecko \textsc{Api} we call'', not a catalog of everything CoinGecko
Inc.\ does, so one kind per source is enough.

Names are canonicalized by \texttt{canonicalize-slug}.

\section{Types}

\lispTypeSubsection{data-source-kind}

A CLOS class with slots \texttt{id}, \texttt{name}, and
\texttt{description}.  The accessors are \lispId{data-source-kind-id},
\lispId{data-source-kind-name}, and \lispId{data-source-kind-description}.
\texttt{data-source-kind-p} is true of instances.

\section{Functions}

\lispFunctionSubsection{standard-data-source-kinds}

The conventional \texttt{(name description)} pairs.  A fresh copy is
returned, so callers may mutate the result freely.

The seeded names are \texttt{api}, \texttt{website}, \texttt{feed},
\texttt{file}, and \texttt{manual}.

\lispFunctionSubsection{make-data-source-kind}

Find or create the data source kind with the given name and return it.
\texttt{:description}, when given, fills in a missing description on an
existing kind.

\lispSyntax

\lispCall{\texttt{(make-data-source-kind \lispVar{name} \&key \lispVar{description})}}

\lispFunctionSubsection{data-source-kind-by-name}

The data source kind whose canonical name is \lispVar{name}, or
\texttt{nil}.  This does not create a row.

\lispFunctionSubsection{data-source-kind-id-for}

The database \textsc{Uuid} of \lispVar{kind}, or \texttt{nil} when
\lispVar{kind} is \texttt{nil}.  \lispVar{kind} may be a
\texttt{data-source-kind} object or a name string (which is found or
created).

\lispFunctionSubsection{all-data-source-kinds}

Every data source kind, alphabetically by name.

\lispFunctionSubsection{ensure-standard-data-source-kinds}

Insert the standard kinds if they are not already present, and return
them.  Call this once after the migrations have been applied.
